  data:  # 8923
    kind: work_item  # 8923
    title: "Tighten drift linter: enforce 'matches canonical axis principle', not just 'in registry'"  # 8923
    status: todo_soft  # 8923
    priority: 4  # 8923
    parent:  # 8923
    tags:  # 8923
    - concept_audit  # 8923
    - agent_infrastructure  # 8923
    - analysis_quality  # 8923
    isbefore: []  # 8923
    duplicate_of: []  # 8923
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 8923
    pr_ref:  # 8923
    description: "The drift linter (find_axis_drift in hypergumbo_core.edge_types, used by both the in-tree property test in test_edge_types.py and the pre-commit CLI scripts/check-edge-type-drift) currently enforces only that consumer-side *EDGE_TYPE* named sets are SUBSETS of the canonical registry — every value referenced by a consumer set must exist in EDGE_TYPES. This is a registry-membership check.\n\nIt does NOT enforce the stronger axis-principle invariant that ADR-0023 establishes: every value should be relationship-shaped (the canonical axis). The linter accepts endpoint_shape and pending_classification values silently, even though they violate the canonical principle. Once ADR-0023 §6 Phase 2/3/4 (siblings WI-sahab-fatoz, WI-mokam-jalig, WI-vomoj-suhaz) lands and endpoint_shape values are removed from the registry, the tightening becomes natural — but it warrants explicit design work because pending_classification handling is a separate question.\n\nCONTEXT (for a dev picking this up cold):\n\nADR-0023 (docs/adr/0023-edge-type-relationship-not-endpoints.md, currently Draft) establishes that edge_type should NAME the relationship between src and dst, not endpoint properties. Values currently classified as endpoint_shape (imports_module, native_bridge, http_calls, etc.) violate this principle and are deprecation candidates. Values at pending_classification (dispatches_to, routes_to, event_publishes, di_resolves, implements_rpc) are deferred per-family audits whose canonical landings have not been decided yet.\n\nPR #3459 landed find_axis_drift with the 'subset of registry' check. PR #3462 added the pre-commit gate. The check has been load-bearing — it caught five silent bugs at PR #3459 close-out (ranking.py, slice.py, taint.py, compact.py, io_boundary.py). The conservative bar was deliberate: during the active migration the linter must not block legitimate transitional code.\n\nAfter Phase 4b (sibling WI-vomoj-suhaz) removes endpoint_shape values from the registry, the registry will contain only relationship + pending_classification. At that point, the existing membership check IMPLICITLY enforces axis-canonical-only for everything except pending_classification — but that subset still warrants a design decision about whether consumer sets should be allowed to reference pending values.\n\nTWO SUB-TIGHTENINGS worth designing together:\n\n1. EXPLICIT AXIS-PRINCIPLE ENFORCEMENT. Even though Phase 4b removes the current endpoint_shape values from the registry, future axis additions (from new ADR-0024-style declarations) might temporarily land new deprecation candidates. The linter should be configurable to enforce 'consumer sets reference only relationship-axis values' so that future deprecation candidates cannot be silently relied upon by consumers.\n\n2. PENDING_CLASSIFICATION HANDLING. Should consumer sets be allowed to reference pending_classification values? Two stances:\n   - Permissive: yes (pending values are still real edge types; consumer code may need them; per-family audit will resolve canonical landings later).\n   - Strict: no (pending values' identity is unsettled by definition; consumers should not rely on them until the family audit resolves them onto the relationship axis).\n   I lean strict because pending IS the signal that the value's identity is unsettled — strict surfaces accidental reliance early. But this is a design question worth explicit user review before committing.\n\nDESIGN OPTIONS:\n\nOption A — Single global toggle. find_axis_drift gains an axis_filter parameter; default is None (current behavior); strict mode passes AXIS_RELATIONSHIP. The CLI scripts/check-edge-type-drift gets a --strict flag. Land the option as one PR; flip the default after Phase 4b lands and per-family audits resolve as a separate PR.\n\nOption B — Per-set axis declaration. Consumer sets self-declare via name suffix or comment annotation: _RELATIONSHIP_EDGE_TYPES = {...} is checked against relationship-only; _ANY_EDGE_TYPES = {...} is checked against all-of-registry. Slightly more intrusive but more flexible — a consumer that legitimately needs to handle both relationship and endpoint_shape values (during a multi-phase rollout) can declare so. Anti-pattern risk: the declarations themselves can drift.\n\nOption C — Just rely on registry composition. Do not add a separate axis check; trust that the registry contains only canonical values after Phase 4b. Pending_classification values stay until per-family audits move them. Simple but loses 'explicit principle enforcement' benefit and provides no protection against future endpoint_shape regressions.\n\nDEPENDENCIES (HARD):\n- WI-sahab-fatoz-zosaj-nopuk-nozon-dosud-mogal-juhim — ADR-0023 §6 Phase 2 (consumers migrated to (edge_type, dst.kind) queries).\n- WI-mokam-jalig-fuhab-gifug-sigij-gotar-sisig-zufoz — ADR-0023 §6 Phase 3 (producers unified).\n- WI-vomoj-suhaz-ruvof-vazop-kigoh-tajog-rilav-pajoj — ADR-0023 §6 Phase 4b (endpoint_shape values removed from registry).\n- ADR-0023 advancement from Draft to Accepted — principle enforcement should land against a stable ADR, not a Draft.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- Decide between Options A / B / C (or a combination); document the choice in this item's discussion thread before implementation.\n- Implement the chosen option; the existing drift property test and pre-commit linter pass; new test cases cover strict-mode behavior; the per-axis filter (if Option A or B is chosen) is parameterizable enough to extend to future axis-bearing fields (Symbol.kind, evidence_type, etc.) per ADR-0024's template.\n- The audit playbook's Step 4 automated-detection-pairing note is updated to reflect 'axis-coherence by default after Phase 4b' (or whatever the chosen design implies).\n- ADR-0024 §Enforcement gains a sentence naming the strict-mode default as the recommended end state once a new axis's migration phases complete.\n\nCONSIDERATIONS:\n- Future-proofing: a per-axis variant of find_axis_drift is what unlocks Symbol.kind, evidence_type, etc. when those declare axes per ADR-0024. Option A's design should anticipate parameterization across multiple fields. The naming convention (e.g., axis_filter parameter, --strict-axis= flag) should be field-agnostic.\n- The tightening should not retroactively break valid pending_classification consumer code — if a consumer legitimately holds a set of pending values for a specific reason, the tightening should give them an opt-in escape (an annotation or per-set override) rather than a hard fail.\n\nSIBLINGS:\n- WI-sahab-fatoz, WI-mokam-jalig, WI-vomoj-suhaz — ADR-0023 §6 Phase 2/3/4. All HARD dependencies.\n- ADR-0023 advancement from Draft — separate item; this tightening should not land while the ADR is Draft.\n- Per-family audit items for dispatch, publish, IPC families — separate items; their resolution affects sub-tightening #2 (pending_classification handling).\n- find_axis_drift scope broadening (currently scans only packages/) — separate item; broader scope means more candidate sets, which interacts with the strictness decision.\n- find_axis_drift name-filter generalization (currently *EDGE_TYPE* substring; doesn't generalize to other axis-bearing fields) — separate item; if Option A/B is chosen, the parameterization should be designed jointly with that generalization to avoid two passes.\n\nBACKGROUND: surfaced as loose-end #19 in the audit-lifecycle session (Apr 2026). The drift linter's 'in the registry' check is conservative to avoid blocking the active migration; the tighter axis-principle check is the intended end state after the migration completes."  # 8923
    fields: {}  # 8923
- op: discuss  # 82e9
  at: '2026-05-01T08:25:30Z'  # 82e9
  by: agent  # 82e9
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 82e9
  clock: 2  # 82e9
  nonce: '82e9'  # 82e9
  message: "DESIGN DECISION (per discussion 2026-05-01): Option A + permissive pending.\n\nCHOSEN APPROACH:\n- find_axis_drift gains axis_filter: frozenset[str] | None = None parameter. When None, current 'subset of registry' check; when a frozenset, additional 'every value's axis is in the filter' check.\n- scripts/check-edge-type-drift gains a --strict flag that passes axis_filter=frozenset({AXIS_RELATIONSHIP, AXIS_PENDING}) — relationship + pending values allowed, endpoint_shape values rejected.\n- Default flips to --strict after Phase 4b (WI-vomoj-suhaz) lands. Pre-Phase-4b: default off (preserves the dual-validity bridge).\n- Future axis migrations get a clean playbook: 'when introducing new endpoint_shape candidates for your axis, temporarily disable strict in your Phase 2 PR; re-enable in your Phase 4b PR.'\n- The axis_filter parameter generalizes naturally to future axis-bearing fields per ADR-0024 (Symbol.kind, evidence_type, etc.) — each declares its own canonical axis to filter on.\n\nWHY OPTION A (over B and C):\n- Over B (per-set name-suffix declaration): simpler to maintain; harder to drift. The per-set name-suffix discipline (e.g., _RELATIONSHIP_EDGE_TYPES vs _ANY_EDGE_TYPES) shifts the bug from 'wrong value in set' to 'wrong name on set' — naming-convention rot. Marginal flexibility benefit doesn't justify the cognitive surface area.\n- Over C (do nothing): real future-regression protection at near-zero cost. The next axis migration that adds deprecation candidates to the registry would silently re-open the door for consumers to reference them under Option C. Option A closes that door explicitly.\n\nWHY PERMISSIVE PENDING (over strict pending):\n- Pending values are REAL EMITTED EDGES from production linkers. resolver_implements / resolver_for_type / openapi_implements / implements_rpc are produced by GraphQL/OpenAPI/gRPC analyzers. Consumers may legitimately need to filter or weight them. io_boundary._TRACEABLE_EDGE_TYPES references implements_rpc TODAY for a real reason.\n- The strict-pending position would block useful consumer work for months pending the resolver/openapi/RPC family audit (analogous to ADR-0025/0026; not yet started).\n- The membership check ALREADY catches the relevant safety failure: when a pending value is renamed by its family audit, it disappears from the registry and any consumer reference becomes a drift hit.\n- Strict pending conflates a coordination concern ('we want to discourage building on shaky values') with a mechanical one ('the linter should enforce it'). The coordination work belongs in the family audit's tracker item.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION CONTRACT (post-Phase-4b execution, ~30 lines + tests):\n\n  # In hypergumbo_core/edge_types.py:\n  def find_axis_drift(\n      repo_root: Path,\n      *,\n      axis_filter: frozenset[str] | None = None,  # NEW\n  ) -> list[str]:\n      ...\n\n  # In scripts/check-edge-type-drift:\n  parser.add_argument('--strict', action='store_true', ...)\n  filter_set = (\n      frozenset({AXIS_RELATIONSHIP, AXIS_PENDING}) if args.strict else None\n  )\n  offenders = find_axis_drift(REPO_ROOT, axis_filter=filter_set)\n\n  # In .githooks/pre-commit (post-Phase-4b):\n  scripts/check-edge-type-drift --strict\n\nDEPENDENCIES STILL OPEN:\n- HARD: Phase 4b (WI-vomoj-suhaz) must land first. Pre-Phase-4b the registry contains 29 endpoint_shape values + intentionally-preserved consumer references (compact.CROSS_CUTTING_EDGE_TYPES, ranking weights, bakeoff-deep, io_boundary, taint). Strict mode would flag all of those, defeating the dual-validity safety net.\n- WI-vomoj-suhaz Phase 4b is itself gated on bakeoff validation clearing (the awaits_bakeoff_validation tags on Phase 2/3 tracker items must drop first).\n\nPOST-EXECUTION ALSO REQUIRED:\n- ADR-0024 §Enforcement gains a sentence naming the strict-mode default as the recommended end state for any axis after its migration phases complete.\n- Audit playbook Step 4 'pair manual audit with automated detection' note updated to reflect 'axis-coherence by default after Phase 4b.'\n\nThis thread is now executable: when WI-vomoj-suhaz Phase 4b lands, this item becomes a clean ~half-day mechanical change."  # 82e9
